# Operator commands are fish

The operator pastes commands into fish. The agent's own shell may be zsh. A command the
operator will copy must parse in fish. A command the agent runs itself may use zsh.

Keep the fence labeled `bash` so the editor still shows a copy button. The contents must
still be valid fish.

## Do not paste bash heredocs

Fish rejects `<<`. The block fails before any line runs, including a `git add` above the
commit:

```bash
git commit -m "$(cat <<'EOF'
Message here.

EOF
)"
```

A one-line message:

```bash
git commit -m 'Message here.'
```

A multiline message is one single-quoted string with a real line break:

```bash
git commit -m 'First line.

Second line.'
```

## Other bash syntax to avoid in operator pastes

- `VAR=value command` — use `env VAR=value command`
- `[[ ... ]]`
- arrays (`arr=(a b)`)

Pipes, `&&`, `$(...)`, and single-quoted strings are fine.

## Agent-run commits

When the agent itself runs `git commit` in its shell, a heredoc is fine. Never put that
heredoc in a block the operator is told to paste.
